{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<p>Not everything needs an LLM. Looking around, checking your inventory or rolling
dice should be <strong>instant, free and exact</strong>. Anything starting with
<code>/</code> is a command handled by plain Python; everything else goes to the
Dungeon Master.</p>

<p>Here is the router at the top of <code>Game.handle</code>:</p>
{{ snippet("game.py", "handle") }}

<h2>A tiny command registry</h2>
<p>A decorator collects commands into a dict. <code>/help</code> is generated from it.</p>
{{ snippet("commands.py", "registry") }}

<h2>/look reads the save, not the LLM</h2>
<p>This matters more than it seems: <code>/look</code> renders the current place straight
from the world data. If the Dungeon Master <em>said</em> there's a goblin here but
<code>/look</code> doesn't show one, the model forgot to save it. Commands are your
lie detector.</p>
{{ snippet("commands.py", "render_look") }}

<h2>/undo</h2>
<p>Before every LLM turn the whole world is serialised to a string. Undo just
restores the last one. Cheap, simple, and it lets players experiment without fear.</p>
{{ snippet("game.py", "undo") }}

<table>
  <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/look /inv /me /map</code></td><td>Read the world</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/journal /facts</code></td><td>Read the history and lore</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/roll 2d6+1</code></td><td>Roll dice yourself</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/undo</code></td><td>Undo the last Dungeon Master turn</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/new [premise] /worlds /load</code></td><td>Manage worlds</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/debug</code></td><td>Show what the LLM did</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="box try">Type "I pick up the mug and smash it" (or similar), then <code>/look</code>,
then <code>/undo</code>, then <code>/look</code> again.</div>

<div class="box exercise">Add a <code>/beings</code> command that lists every character in
the world and where they are.</div>
{% endblock %}
